※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=35 diag=none

※ The questions about a piece of the program.  `⎕head` is what it is made by,
※ `⎕parts` how many pieces it applies its head to, and `⎕part` which one -- and a
※ piece compared with `=` asks whether the same thing is written in both, wherever
※ each was written.
※
※ An operator alone between the marks is the operator itself rather than an
※ expression using it, which is what `⎕head` of a sum answers.  Nothing about any
※ particular operator is built in: finding a sum is a macro asking whether the head
※ is `+`.
※
※ `⎕part` names which part it wants instead of answering them all at once, which is
※ what keeps an array out of the machine that runs a macro -- how many there are is
※ its own question and a walk over them is a loop the macro writes.

macro sum(e: syntax) → syntax:
    if ⎕head(e) = ⌜+⌝:
        ⌜$(⎕part(e, 0u64)) × 10u8⌝
    else:
        ⌜$e⌝

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(sum⌜3u8 + 4u8⌝ + sum⌜5u8⌝, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
